TWO HALVES, AND THE ITEM NAMED ONLY ONE. cpp.py computes is_member_call (it
must, to suppress the STL fallback) and discarded it, so gate_named_entry's
'call_construct == "method"' refusal was unreachable for the language: 1 of
34,983 cpp call edges on whisper.cpp carried the value. But stamping it is
INERT on its own -- measured, zero boundaries moved -- because the filed FP
never reaches that gate. cpp.py sets the module slot to the file's whole
#include set, which tests as a usable hint, so lookup_with_module returns from
its module-filter branch, which never reads the construct.

THE OBVIOUS CONSUMER RULE IS WRONG AND WAS MEASURED BEFORE BEING REVERTED.
Refusing function-kind whenever the construct is 'method' removed 51 matches on
whisper.cpp: the 2 targets and 49 TRUE positives (os.Open, os.Stat,
fmt.Fprintln, net/http.NewRequest, logging.exception). call_construct ==
'method' does not mean 'instance method' -- Go spells a package-qualified
function call as a selector expression, identical in shape to an instance call,
and stamps both. A definite module slot is exactly what disambiguates those,
which is why the F3 gate defers to it.

So the refusal is keyed on the slot's ARITY, which _module_hint_candidates
already computes: os / fmt / java.io.File expand to one candidate and are
untouched; a cpp #include set expands to six. A disjunction is an uncertainty
set -- cpp.py's own comment says 'this call could be from any of the included
headers' -- so it is no better than no hint, and the construct rule applies.

